## Small Business

Use of the software for the benefit of your company is use for a permitted
purpose if your company has:

1. fewer than 100 total individuals working as employees and independent
   contractors;
2. less than 1,000,000 USD (2026) in total revenue in the prior tax year;
   and
3. less than 5,000,000 USD (2026) in total assets at the end of the prior
   tax year.

All three conditions must hold. The amounts are adjusted annually for
inflation according to the United States Bureau of Labor Statistics'
consumer price index for all urban consumers, U.S. city average, for all
items, not seasonally adjusted, with 1982–1984 = 100 reference base.

Counting employees, independent contractors, revenue and assets, include
those of every company that controls your company, that your company
controls, and that is under common control with your company.

## Noncommercial Use

Personal use for research, experiment and testing for the benefit of public
knowledge, personal study, private entertainment, hobby projects, amateur
pursuits, or religious observance, without any anticipated commercial
application, is use for a permitted purpose. Use by any charitable
organization, educational institution, public research organization, public
safety or health organization, environmental protection organization, or
government institution is use for a permitted purpose regardless of the
source of funding or obligations resulting from the funding.

## Commercial License Above the Limits

If your company does not meet every condition under Small Business, and its
use is not a permitted purpose under Noncommercial Use, you must obtain a
commercial license from Denev Operations EOOD before using the software. Use
of the software for evaluation, for up to 30 days, is a permitted purpose
for any company.

## Growing or Joining a Larger Organization

If your company stops meeting the conditions under Small Business, and its
use is not otherwise a permitted purpose, your license will not stop
immediately. You have 90 days from the date your company stopped meeting
those conditions to obtain a commercial license from the licensor or stop
using the software. Your license ends at the end of that period unless you
have obtained a commercial license.

